<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chieti</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Teate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chieti is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on a hill above the Pescara river near the Adriatic. As Teate it was the main town of the Italic Marrucini and later a Roman municipium, with temples, baths and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name gave the Theatine order its name, as its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iv">Gian Pietro Carafa</a>, later Pope Paul IV, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Chieti.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chieti.jpg" alt="Chieti" width="320" height="152" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ra Boe · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chieti_2008_by-RaBoe_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chieti is a city and comune in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a>, 200 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and the capital of the province of Chieti. It lies about 10 kilometres from the Adriatic Sea, with the Majella and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gran-sasso">Gran Sasso</a> mountains in the background. The old town, Chieti Alta, stands on a hill surrounded by the Pescara and Alento rivers. Its inhabitants are called teatini, and the English form of that name survives in the Theatines, a Catholic religious order.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Legend says the city was founded by the companions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a> and named for his mother Thetis, and according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> it was founded by Arcadians as Thegeate. Around the 10th century BCE the Marrucini, an Italic tribe probably from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabina">Sabina</a>, took the territory from the Osci, and Teate Marrucinorum was their chief town. After defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> the Marrucini became loyal allies. They received Roman citizenship and municipal status after the Social War, in which the Teate general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herius-asinius">Herius Asinius</a> was killed. Teate flourished in the first century BCE as a municipium.</p><p>In 801 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-of-italy">Pepin of Italy</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, destroyed the town, which remained a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> for two centuries. In the 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> opened a consulate there in 1555, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iv">Gian Pietro Carafa</a>, the future Pope Paul IV, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and archbishop. In 1861 it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Chieti has Roman remains: three temples, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, baths with mosaics, underground cisterns, and a citadel with an amphitheatre of the first century CE. The National Archaeology Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a> houses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warrior-of-capestrano">Warrior of Capestrano</a>. The cathedral, dedicated to Saint Justin, was probably founded in the 8th century and keeps parts of a Romanesque crypt. Its Good Friday procession is held by some historians to have taken place since 842.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chieti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Marrucini occupied a narrow strip between the Aterno river and the Foro, inland of the Adriatic coast in modern Abruzzo, with <b>Teate</b> as their main town.</blockquote><a href="/state/marrucini">Marrucini</a></li></ul></section>
